00:01:48,500 --> 00:01:55,840
It all takes place in the little town of Epsom, New Hampshire, population 5,000.

39
00:01:55,840 --> 00:01:59,960
And in this little town, on the side of a little mountain, there's a Christmas tree

40
00:01:59,960 --> 00:02:01,200
farm.

41
00:02:01,200 --> 00:02:09,320
Everyone I knew, if you were local in Epsom, you knew of the tree farm.

42
00:02:09,320 --> 00:02:11,260
This is Katie Bartocci.

43
00:02:11,260 --> 00:02:15,180
In the Hallmark movie, she'd play one of the supporting characters, the voice of the

44
00:02:15,180 --> 00:02:16,180
town.

45
00:02:16,180 --> 00:02:20,340
We always got our Christmas tree there. My family always got their Christmas tree there.

46
00:02:20,340 --> 00:02:22,740
My sisters always got their Christmas tree there.

47
00:02:23,540 --> 00:02:27,940
Katie grew up on a road that leads to the Christmas tree farm. Every year she watched

48
00:02:27,940 --> 00:02:33,380
cars drive by with freshly cut trees strapped on top. So I'd be like, oh, somebody else got a tree,

49
00:02:33,380 --> 00:02:37,300
somebody else got a tree. And it was like, come on, like people would be coming up and down the road

50
00:02:37,300 --> 00:02:42,980
with trees. To get to the tree farm, you take a dirt road through the woods and up the side of the

51
00:02:42,980 --> 00:02:48,260
the mountain. High enough to make your ears pop. You get to the top of it and it's just like

52
00:02:48,260 --> 00:02:52,900
the weather was always so crazy. I remember every time we went up there you were there like if it

53
00:02:52,900 --> 00:02:59,780
was 50 degrees down where I lived it was like 20 degrees up there. When you reach the end of the

54
00:02:59,780 --> 00:03:05,620
road the farm is on either side of you. Fields of grass with neat rows of Christmas trees of

55
00:03:05,620 --> 00:03:10,340
different heights. And then there's everything you'd expect from a classic New England farm.

56
00:03:10,340 --> 00:03:16,300
a stone wall, a barn, and a farmhouse that's older than the Constitution.

57
00:03:16,300 --> 00:03:24,340
And it's in that farmhouse that the Hallmark movies main characters live.

58
00:03:24,340 --> 00:03:32,120
Patricia and John Hickey, Patty and Jay. If the tree farm was an institution in

59
00:03:32,120 --> 00:03:38,640
Epsom, so were Patty and Jay. Patty was the town's elected welfare officer for

60
00:03:38,640 --> 00:03:44,940
more than 30 years. Jay was even more civically involved. Over the course of his life, it

61
00:03:44,940 --> 00:03:50,160
seemed like he'd held just about every single post in town government, from the rescue squad

62
00:03:50,160 --> 00:03:55,720
to the select board. Add to that assistant coach of the local baseball team, which was

63
00:03:55,720 --> 00:04:01,480
sponsored by the Christmas Tree Farm. In the fall, Patty and Jay would hire local kids

64
00:04:01,480 --> 00:04:06,320
to help out for the Christmas season. Here's Owen Thomas and Colby Kalman.

65
00:04:06,320 --> 00:04:08,760
I mean, honestly, it wasn't too much.

66
00:04:08,760 --> 00:04:11,480
We would just, they had like a little car

67
00:04:11,480 --> 00:04:14,680
and they'd just keep that on all day, keep us heated.

68
00:04:14,680 --> 00:04:16,760
And you know, my buddies would just kind of sit there

69
00:04:16,760 --> 00:04:19,040
and play games and stuff until a car came up.

70
00:04:19,040 --> 00:04:21,840
Sometimes Jay would come out and talk to them.

71
00:04:21,840 --> 00:04:25,160
He knew, it seemed like he knew a lot about the trees.

72
00:04:26,320 --> 00:04:29,320
He would go and start talking about his blue spruces

73
00:04:29,320 --> 00:04:31,920
and he would really get into the work

74
00:04:31,920 --> 00:04:33,740
that he was putting in in the summertime

75
00:04:33,740 --> 00:04:34,960
to make it all happen.

76
00:04:34,960 --> 00:04:39,640
and I'll tell you, he's very, very passionate

77
00:04:39,640 --> 00:04:40,800
about Christmas trees.

78
00:04:44,440 --> 00:04:46,520
The farm was Jay's baby.

79
00:04:46,520 --> 00:04:49,160
He'd owned it since the early 1970s.

80
00:04:49,160 --> 00:04:51,480
Patty moved in about a decade later,

81
00:04:51,480 --> 00:04:54,920
and together they had a blended family of five kids.

82
00:04:56,240 --> 00:05:00,320
Jay had a big fluffy mustache that swept down and out

83
00:05:00,320 --> 00:05:01,720
on either side of his mouth,

84
00:05:01,720 --> 00:05:04,480
like two wings taking flight on his face.

85
00:05:04,960 --> 00:05:07,960
Patty wore glasses and had long straight hair.

86
00:05:08,960 --> 00:05:12,960
She's always got a smile, always, always, always had a smile on her face.

87
00:05:12,960 --> 00:05:16,960
She'd talk to the wall if you let her.

88
00:05:16,960 --> 00:05:20,960
Patty was, he'd borderline Mrs. Claus, if you will.

89
00:05:21,960 --> 00:05:24,960
Mrs. Claus and the Christmas tree farmer.

90
00:05:24,960 --> 00:05:27,960
The movie practically writes itself.

91
00:05:29,960 --> 00:05:32,960
Jay and Patty grow old together on McCoy Mountain,

92
00:05:32,960 --> 00:05:36,160
surrounded by their blue spruce Christmas trees.

93
00:05:36,160 --> 00:05:37,960
They reach their mid-70s.

94
00:05:37,960 --> 00:05:39,440
It's just the two of them now.

95
00:05:39,440 --> 00:05:42,160
Their kids have long since grown up and moved away.

96
00:05:52,600 --> 00:05:54,480
And then things go off script.

97
00:05:56,120 --> 00:05:58,720
Jay starts having memory issues.

98
00:05:58,720 --> 00:06:00,280
He stops driving.

99
00:06:00,280 --> 00:06:06,200
In 2020, he retires from his latest post as the town's zoning compliance officer.

100
00:06:06,200 --> 00:06:11,380
Meadow Isaki worked with Jay in town government at the time.

101
00:06:11,380 --> 00:06:15,340
When we noticed the decline he started withdrawing, I mean, I think that was probably the first

102
00:06:15,340 --> 00:06:23,060
thing that I saw was that when you were around him, he was very hesitant to say things to

103
00:06:23,060 --> 00:06:27,780
people like he wasn't quite sure if he was following what was being discussed or that

104
00:06:27,780 --> 00:06:30,060
what he was going to say was going to be appropriate.

105
00:06:30,060 --> 00:06:31,940
So I think she just kind of shut down.

106
00:06:33,020 --> 00:06:35,740
Jay is diagnosed with dementia.

107
00:06:35,740 --> 00:06:37,460
Patty becomes his caretaker,

108
00:06:38,580 --> 00:06:41,500
which she now has to juggle alongside her role

109
00:06:41,500 --> 00:06:43,300
as the town welfare officer.

110
00:06:44,260 --> 00:06:46,180
The job involves helping out residents

111
00:06:46,180 --> 00:06:48,500
who've fallen on hard times with loans

112
00:06:48,500 --> 00:06:50,540
or other assistance programs.

113
00:06:50,540 --> 00:06:52,780
If you suddenly can't afford to pay your heating bill

114
00:06:52,780 --> 00:06:56,620
in the dead of winter, Patty was the person to call.

115
00:06:56,620 --> 00:06:59,940
She would put her office hours around times

116
00:06:59,940 --> 00:07:01,780
that she could bring him in with her

117
00:07:01,780 --> 00:07:05,020
so that she could keep track of him and stuff.

118
00:07:05,020 --> 00:07:07,180
And I know when we were doing our move

119
00:07:07,180 --> 00:07:11,060
from the former office's location

120
00:07:11,060 --> 00:07:12,860
to the current office location

121
00:07:12,860 --> 00:07:16,540
that he had attended with her to help with pack

122
00:07:16,540 --> 00:07:18,940
or when she was packing her office.

123
00:07:18,940 --> 00:07:22,180
But she had to leave because he kind of wandered off

124
00:07:22,180 --> 00:07:24,660
because she couldn't keep track of him really closely

125
00:07:24,660 --> 00:07:25,980
and he was out in front of the building

126
00:07:25,980 --> 00:07:27,180
